ax-width,40rem))] origin-top-left flex-col gap-2 overflow-x-clip" style="opacity: 1; transform: none;"><span class="" data-state="closed"><div class="relative w-full text-start"><div class="flex w-full items-start justify-between text-start flex-col"><button class="flex w-full items-center gap-0.5"><span class=""><span class="flex items-center gap-1 text-start align-middle truncate text-token-text-secondary hover:text-token-text-primary" style="opacity: 1;">Thought for 10s</span></span></button></div></div></span></div></div><div data-message-author-role="assistant" data-message-id="25789ae2-a4a8-4f51-aef1-6c18bf3650d5" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-t-mini"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="532">Good — below I’ll show, in clear steps, how to implement <strong data-start="57" data-end="78">if-then pipelines</strong> and <strong data-start="83" data-end="101">decision trees</strong> using your NAND-only hardware base. I include (1) the conceptual patterns, (2) concrete NAND-only realizations for the key building blocks (mux and comparator), (3) two canonical ways to map decision logic (serial/priority and binary/hierarchical), and (4) how to pipeline those maps (registering, control signals, hazards). No diagrams; everything is expressed as logic recipes and practical rules you can implement gate-by-gate.</p>
<hr data-start="534" data-end="537">
<h1 data-start="539" data-end="567">0. Short intuitive summary</h1>
<ul data-start="568" data-end="1116">
<li data-start="568" data-end="664">
<p data-start="570" data-end="664">An <code data-start="573" data-end="590">if (P) A else B</code> is just a <strong data-start="601" data-end="612">2:1 mux</strong> that selects <code data-start="626" data-end="629">A</code> when predicate <code data-start="645" data-end="648">P</code> true, else <code data-start="660" data-end="663">B</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="665" data-end="840">
<p data-start="667" data-end="840">Chains of <code data-start="677" data-end="691">if/elif/else</code> can be implemented either as <strong data-start="721" data-end="739">priority logic</strong> (priority encoder + mux) or as <strong data-start="771" data-end="796">binary decision trees</strong> (hierarchical comparators + tree of muxes).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="841" data-end="1004">
<p data-start="843" data-end="1004">Pipelining = cut the combinational decision network into stages and insert registers (D-FFs). Provide <code data-start="945" data-end="952">valid</code> and <code data-start="957" data-end="964">stall</code> or <code data-start="968" data-end="975">flush</code> control to keep correctness.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1005" data-end="1116">
<p data-start="1007" data-end="1116">Everything reduces to NAND: build NOT, AND, OR, XOR, comparators, registers from NAND and wire them together.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1118" data-end="1121">
<h1 data-start="1123" data-end="1177">1. Basic NAND-only building blocks (compact recipes)</h1>
<h3 data-start="1179" data-end="1211">1.1 NOT, AND, OR (NAND only)</h3>
<ul data-start="1212" data-end="1368">
<li data-start="1212" data-end="1235">
<p data-start="1214" data-end="1235"><code data-start="1214" data-end="1234">NOT(x) = NAND(x,x)</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1236" data-end="1299">
<p data-start="1238" data-end="1299"><code data-start="1238" data-end="1298">AND(a,b) = NOT( NAND(a,b) ) = NAND( NAND(a,b), NAND(a,b) )</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1300" data-end="1368">
<p data-start="1302" data-end="1368"><code data-start="1302" data-end="1367">OR(a,b) = NAND( NOT(a), NOT(b) ) = NAND( NAND(a,a), NAND(b,b) )</code>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="1370" data-end="1421">1.2 2:1 MUX (single-bit) implemented with NANDs</h3>
<p data-start="1422" data-end="1430">We want</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="true">‾</mo></mover><mo>∧</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∨</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>∧</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y = (\overline{S}\land A) \lor (S\land B).</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.22222em;">Y</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.1333em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord overline"><span class="vlist-t"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8833em;"><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span></span></span><span style="top: -3.8033em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="overline-line" style="border-bottom-width: 0.04em;"></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∧</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">A</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∨</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05764em;">S</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∧</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.05017em;">B</span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="1480" data-end="1513">NAND-only construction (4 NANDs):</p>
<ol data-start="1514" data-end="1700">
<li data-start="1514" data-end="1548">
<p data-start="1517" data-end="1548"><code data-start="1517" data-end="1548">N1 = NAND(S,S)        // = ¬S</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1549" data-end="1590">
<p data-start="1552" data-end="1590"><code data-start="1552" data-end="1590">N2 = NAND(N1, A)      // = ¬(¬S ∧ A)</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1591" data-end="1631">
<p data-start="1594" data-end="1631"><code data-start="1594" data-end="1631">N3 = NAND(S, B)       // = ¬(S ∧ B)</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1632" data-end="1700">
<p data-start="1635" data-end="1700"><code data-start="1635" data-end="1700">Y  = NAND(N2, N3)     // = ¬(¬(¬S∧A) ∧ ¬(S∧B)) = (¬S∧A) ∨ (S∧B)</code></p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start="1702" data-end="1766">This is compact and practical; replicate per bit for wide buses.</p>
<h3 data-start="1768" data-end="1816">1.3 XNOR / equality (useful for comparators)</h3>
<p data-start="1817" data-end="1887"><code data-start="1817" data-end="1854">EQ = (a == b) = (a ∧ b) ∨ (¬a ∧ ¬b)</code>. NAND network (6 NANDs typical):</p>
<ol data-start="1888" data-end="2075">
<li data-start="1888" data-end="1924">
<p data-start="1891" data-end="1924"><code data-start="1891" data-end="1907">t1 = NAND(a,b)</code>        // ¬(a∧b)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1925" data-end="1957">
<p data-start="1928" data-end="1957"><code data-start="1928" data-end="1944">na = NAND(a,a)</code>        // ¬a</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1958" data-end="1990">
<p data-start="1961" data-end="1990"><code data-start="1961" data-end="1977">nb = NAND(b,b)</code>        // ¬b</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1991" data-end="2029">
<p data-start="1994" data-end="2029"><code data-start="1994" data-end="2012">t2 = NAND(na,nb)</code>      // ¬(¬a∧¬b)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2030" data-end="2075">
<p data-start="2033" data-end="2075"><code data-start="2033" data-end="2051">EQ = NAND(t1,t2)</code>      // (a∧b) ∨ (¬a∧¬b)</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h3 data-start="2077" data-end="2117">1.4 Greater-than (MSB-first cascade)</h3>
<p data-start="2118" data-end="2191">To build <code data-start="2127" data-end="2136">GT(a,b)</code> for <code data-start="2141" data-end="2144">w</code>-bit unsigned values, use MSB-to-LSB recursion:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∧</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo>∨</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∧</mo><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GT_w = (a_{w-1}\land\overline{b_{w-1}}) \;\lor\; ( (a_{w-1}==b_{w-1}) \land GT_{w-1} ).</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.8333em; vertical-align: -0.15em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">G</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.1389em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.15em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">(</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span><span class="mbin mtight">−</span><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2083em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∧</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1.1444em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord overline"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.8944em;"><span style="top: -3em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">b</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span><span class="mbin mtight">−</span><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2083em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span style="top: -3.8144em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 3em;"></span><span class="overline-line" style="border-bottom-width: 0.04em;"></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2083em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∨</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mopen">((</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">a</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span><span class="mbin mtight">−</span><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2083em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">==</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal">b</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: 0em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span><span class="mbin mtight">−</span><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2083em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">∧</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">G</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.3011em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.1389em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight"><span class="mord mtight"><span class="mord mathnormal mtight" style="margin-right: 0.02691em;">w</span><span class="mbin mtight">−</span><span class="mord mtight">1</span></span></span></span></span><span class="vlist-s">​</span></span><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.2083em;"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mclose">)</span><span class="mord">.</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="2286" data-end="2436">Implement each AND/OR/XNOR using NAND recipes above and recurse down; combinational depth is <code data-start="2379" data-end="2385">O(w)</code> but you can restructure for parallelism if needed.</p>
<hr data-start="2438" data-end="2441">
<h1 data-start="2443" data-end="2486">2. Mapping <code data-start="2456" data-end="2460">if</code> / decision logic to gates</h1>
<h3 data-start="2488" data-end="2527">2.1 Single <code data-start="2503" data-end="2507">if</code> (<code data-start="2509" data-end="2526">if (P) A else B</code>)</h3>
<ul data-start="2528" data-end="2788">
<li data-start="2528" data-end="2666">
<p data-start="2530" data-end="2666">Evaluate predicate <code data-start="2549" data-end="2552">P</code> (comparator, test bit, etc.). <code data-start="2583" data-end="2586">P</code> is a single-bit signal produced by combinational NAND logic (e.g., comparator).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2667" data-end="2788">
<p data-start="2669" data-end="2788">Use the 2:1 NAND mux above to choose between <code data-start="2714" data-end="2717">A</code> and <code data-start="2722" data-end="2725">B</code>. For multi-bit <code data-start="2741" data-end="2746">A,B</code>, replicate that mux per bit (shared <code data-start="2783" data-end="2786">S</code>).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="2790" data-end="2835">2.2 Chained <code data-start="2806" data-end="2824">if / elif / else</code> (priority)</h3>
<p data-start="2836" data-end="2844">Example:</p>
<pre class="overflow-visible!" data-start="2845" data-end="2908"><div class="contain-inline-size rounded-2xl relative bg-token-sidebar-surface-primary"><div class="flex items-center text-token-text-secondary px-4 py-2 text-xs font-sans justify-between h-9 bg-token-sidebar-surface-primary select-none rounded-t-2xl">nginx</div><div class="sticky top-9"><div class="absolute end-0 bottom-0 flex h-9 items-center pe-2"><div class="bg-token-bg-elevated-secondary text-token-text-secondary flex items-center gap-4 rounded-sm px-2 font-sans text-xs"><button class="flex gap-1 items-center select-none py-1" aria-label="Copy"><svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="icon-sm"><path d="M12.668 10.667C12.668 9.95614 12.668 9.46258 12.6367 9.0791C12.6137 8.79732 12.5758 8.60761 12.5244 8.46387L12.4688 8.33399C12.3148 8.03193 12.0803 7.77885 11.793 7.60254L11.666 7.53125C11.508 7.45087 11.2963 7.39395 10.9209 7.36328C10.5374 7.33197 10.0439 7.33203 9.33301 7.33203H6.5C5.78896 7.33203 5.29563 7.33195 4.91211 7.36328C4.63016 7.38632 4.44065 7.42413 4.29688 7.47559L4.16699 7.53125C3.86488 7.68518 3.61186 7.9196 3.43555 8.20703L3.36524 8.33399C3.28478 8.49198 3.22795 8.70352 3.19727 9.0791C3.16595 9.46259 3.16504 9.95611 3.16504 10.667V13.5C3.16504 14.211 3.16593 14.7044 3.19727 15.0879C3.22797 15.4636 3.28473 15.675 3.36524 15.833L3.43555 15.959C3.61186 16.2466 3.86474 16.4807 4.16699 16.6348L4.29688 16.6914C4.44063 16.7428 4.63025 16.7797 4.91211 16.8027C5.29563 16.8341 5.78896 16.835 6.5 16.835H9.33301C10.0439 16.835 10.5374 16.8341 10.9209 16.8027C11.2965 16.772 11.508 16.7152 11.666 16.6348L11.793 16.5645C12.0804 16.3881 12.3148 16.1351 12.4688 15.833L12.5244 15.7031C12.5759 15.5594 12.6137 15.3698 12.6367 15.0879C12.6681 14.7044 12.668 14.211 12.668 13.5V10.667ZM13.998 12.665C14.4528 12.6634 14.8011 12.6602 15.0879 12.6367C15.4635 12.606 15.675 12.5492 15.833 12.4688L15.959 12.3975C16.2466 12.2211 16.4808 11.9682 16.6348 11.666L16.6914 11.5361C16.7428 11.3924 16.7797 11.2026 16.8027 10.9209C16.8341 10.5374 16.835 10.0439 16.835 9.33301V6.5C16.835 5.78896 16.8341 5.29563 16.8027 4.91211C16.7797 4.63025 16.7428 4.44063 16.6914 4.29688L16.6348 4.16699C16.4807 3.86474 16.2466 3.61186 15.959 3.43555L15.833 3.36524C15.675 3.28473 15.4636 3.22797 15.0879 3.19727C14.7044 3.16593 14.211 3.16504 13.5 3.16504H10.667C9.9561 3.16504 9.46259 3.16595 9.0791 3.19727C8.79739 3.22028 8.6076 3.2572 8.46387 3.30859L8.33399 3.36524C8.03176 3.51923 7.77886 3.75343 7.60254 4.04102L7.53125 4.16699C7.4508 4.32498 7.39397 4.53655 7.36328 4.91211C7.33985 5.19893 7.33562 5.54719 7.33399 6.00195H9.33301C10.022 6.00195 10.5791 6.00131 11.0293 6.03809C11.4873 6.07551 11.8937 6.15471 12.2705 6.34668L12.4883 6.46875C12.984 6.7728 13.3878 7.20854 13.6533 7.72949L13.7197 7.87207C13.8642 8.20859 13.9292 8.56974 13.9619 8.9707C13.9987 9.42092 13.998 9.97799 13.998 10.667V12.665ZM18.165 9.33301C18.165 10.022 18.1657 10.5791 18.1289 11.0293C18.0961 11.4302 18.0311 11.7914 17.8867 12.1279L17.8203 12.2705C17.5549 12.7914 17.1509 13.2272 16.6553 13.5313L16.4365 13.6533C16.0599 13.8452 15.6541 13.9245 15.1963 13.9619C14.8593 13.9895 14.4624 13.9935 13.9951 13.9951C13.9935 14.4624 13.9895 14.8593 13.9619 15.1963C13.9292 15.597 13.864 15.9576 13.7197 16.2939L13.6533 16.4365C13.3878 16.9576 12.9841 17.3941 12.4883 17.6982L12.2705 17.8203C11.8937 18.0123 11.4873 18.0915 11.0293 18.1289C10.5791 18.1657 10.022 18.165 9.33301 18.165H6.5C5.81091 18.165 5.25395 18.1657 4.80371 18.1289C4.40306 18.0962 4.04235 18.031 3.70606 17.8867L3.56348 17.8203C3.04244 17.5548 2.60585 17.151 2.30176 16.6553L2.17969 16.4365C1.98788 16.0599 1.90851 15.6541 1.87109 15.1963C1.83431 14.746 1.83496 14.1891 1.83496 13.5V10.667C1.83496 9.978 1.83432 9.42091 1.87109 8.9707C1.90851 8.5127 1.98772 8.10625 2.17969 7.72949L2.30176 7.51172C2.60586 7.0159 3.04236 6.6122 3.56348 6.34668L3.70606 6.28027C4.04237 6.136 4.40303 6.07083 4.80371 6.03809C5.14051 6.01057 5.53708 6.00551 6.00391 6.00391C6.00551 5.53708 6.01057 5.14051 6.03809 4.80371C6.0755 4.34588 6.15483 3.94012 6.34668 3.56348L6.46875 3.34473C6.77282 2.84912 7.20856 2.44514 7.72949 2.17969L7.87207 2.11328C8.20855 1.96886 8.56979 1.90385 8.9707 1.87109C9.42091 1.83432 9.978 1.83496 10.667 1.83496H13.5C14.1891 1.83496 14.746 1.83431 15.1963 1.87109C15.6541 1.90851 16.0599 1.98788 16.4365 2.17969L16.6553 2.30176C17.151 2.60585 17.5548 3.04244 17.8203 3.56348L17.8867 3.70606C18.031 4.04235 18.0962 4.40306 18.1289 4.80371C18.1657 5.25395 18.165 5.81091 18.165 6.5V9.33301Z"></path></svg>Copy code</button></div></div></div><div class="overflow-y-auto p-4" dir="ltr"><code class="whitespace-pre!"><span><span><span class="hljs-attribute">if</span></span><span> (P0) R = R0;
</span><span><span class="hljs-attribute">else</span></span><span> if (P1) R = R1;
</span><span><span class="hljs-attribute">else</span></span><span>       R = R2;
</span></span></code></div></div></pre>
<p data-start="2909" data-end="2929">Two implementations:</p>
<p data-start="2931" data-end="2993"><strong data-start="2931" data-end="2993">A — Priority (sequential checks / priority encoder + mux):</strong></p>
<ul data-start="2994" data-end="3393">
<li data-start="2994" data-end="3092">
<p data-start="2996" data-end="3018">Build one-hot signals:</p>
<ul data-start="3021" data-end="3092">
<li data-start="3021" data-end="3034">
<p data-start="3023" data-end="3034"><code data-start="3023" data-end="3034">sel0 = P0</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start="3037" data-end="3056">
<p data-start="3039" data-end="3056"><code data-start="3039" data-end="3056">sel1 = ¬P0 ∧ P1</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start="3059" data-end="3092">
<p data-start="3061" data-end="3092"><code data-start="3061" data-end="3079">sel2 = ¬P0 ∧ ¬P1</code> (or default)</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="3093" data-end="3329">
<p data-start="3095" data-end="3167">Use a decoder or direct gating to produce one-hot <code data-start="3145" data-end="3152">sel_i</code>. Then compute:</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi><mn>0</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mi>R</mi><mn>0</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>  </mtext><mo>+</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mi>s</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R = sel0\cdot R0 \;+\; sel1\cdot R1 \;+\; sel2\cdot R2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mrel">=</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">se</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.01968em;">l</span><span class="mord">0</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">⋅</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7667em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span><span class="mord">0</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">se</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.01968em;">l</span><span class="mord">1</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">⋅</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.7667em; vertical-align: -0.0833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span><span class="mord">1</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">+</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2778em;"></span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6944em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">se</span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.01968em;">l</span><span class="mord">2</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span><span class="mbin">⋅</span><span class="mspace" style="margin-right: 0.2222em;"></span></span><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 0.6833em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.00773em;">R</span><span class="mord">2</span></span></span></span></span>
<p data-start="3237" data-end="3329">Implement <code data-start="3247" data-end="3252">sel</code> generation and the final OR/AND with NAND recipes (decoder using NAND-tree).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3330" data-end="3393">
<p data-start="3332" data-end="3393">Advantage: natural mapping from <code data-start="3364" data-end="3373">if/elif</code> priority semantics.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="3395" data-end="3430"><strong data-start="3395" data-end="3430">B — Binary tree of comparisons:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="3431" data-end="3644">
<li data-start="3431" data-end="3495">
<p data-start="3433" data-end="3495">Convert chain to tree of binary decisions (balance for depth).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3496" data-end="3585">
<p data-start="3498" data-end="3585">Each internal node uses predicate and a 2:1 mux to route one of two sub-results upward.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3586" data-end="3644">
<p data-start="3588" data-end="3644">Advantage: balanced latency (log N) if tree is balanced.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="3646" data-end="3707">2.3 Decision tree (multi-way based on numeric thresholds)</h3>
<p data-start="3708" data-end="3775">Example: classify input <code data-start="3732" data-end="3735">x</code> into bins <code data-start="3746" data-end="3774">[0..t0), [t0..t1), [t1..∞)</code>.</p>
<ul data-start="3776" data-end="4017">
<li data-start="3776" data-end="3850">
<p data-start="3778" data-end="3850">Evaluate comparators <code data-start="3799" data-end="3807">x &lt; t0</code>, <code data-start="3809" data-end="3817">x &lt; t1</code>, etc., producing predicate bits.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3851" data-end="3953">
<p data-start="3853" data-end="3953">Build selection logic (combinational tree or priority encoder) to produce one-hot selection <code data-start="3945" data-end="3952">sel_i</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3954" data-end="4017">
<p data-start="3956" data-end="4017">Use wide <code data-start="3965" data-end="3970">N:1</code> mux to pick the corresponding output / action.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="4019" data-end="4050">Two useful micro-architectures:</p>
<ul data-start="4051" data-end="4366">
<li data-start="4051" data-end="4189">
<p data-start="4053" data-end="4189"><strong data-start="4053" data-end="4091">Comparator cascade + priority mux:</strong> compute <code data-start="4100" data-end="4108">x &lt; t0</code> first, if true choose bin0; else test <code data-start="4147" data-end="4151">t1</code> etc. (serial). Simple, minimal gates.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4190" data-end="4366">
<p data-start="4192" data-end="4366"><strong data-start="4192" data-end="4240">Parallel comparators + one-hot decode + mux:</strong> compute all comparisons in parallel, decode to one-hot, then a single wide mux chooses result. Higher throughput, more gates.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="4368" data-end="4371">
<h1 data-start="4373" data-end="4407">3. Pipelining the decision logic</h1>
<p data-start="4409" data-end="4496">Pipelining increases throughput by splitting combinational depth across clocked stages.</p>
<h3 data-start="4498" data-end="4538">3.1 Pipeline stages — general recipe</h3>
<ol data-start="4539" data-end="5185">
<li data-start="4539" data-end="4770">
<p data-start="4542" data-end="4653"><strong data-start="4542" data-end="4581">Partition the combinational network</strong> into stages such that each stage’s logic depth fits timing constraints.</p>
<ul data-start="4657" data-end="4770">
<li data-start="4657" data-end="4770">
<p data-start="4659" data-end="4770">Typical cut points: predicate generation (comparators), selection logic (priority/encoder), final multiplexing.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="4771" data-end="4945">
<p data-start="4774" data-end="4945"><strong data-start="4774" data-end="4824">Insert D flip-flops (edge-triggered registers)</strong> on the buses crossing stages. D-FFs are implemented from NAND primitives (classic cross-coupled NAND master-slave pair).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4946" data-end="5090">
<p data-start="4949" data-end="5090"><strong data-start="4949" data-end="4975">Carry control signals:</strong> <code data-start="4976" data-end="4983">valid</code>, <code data-start="4985" data-end="4992">stall</code>, and <code data-start="4998" data-end="5005">flush</code> bits travel with the data so the downstream logic knows if the result is meaningful.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5091" data-end="5185">
<p data-start="5094" data-end="5185"><strong data-start="5094" data-end="5118">Clocking discipline:</strong> synchronous clock; registers capture stage outputs on clock edges.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<h3 data-start="5187" data-end="5239">3.2 Example 3-stage pipeline for a decision tree</h3>
<ul data-start="5240" data-end="5766">
<li data-start="5240" data-end="5359">
<p data-start="5242" data-end="5359"><strong data-start="5242" data-end="5254">Stage 1:</strong> Input sampling + compute all comparators / predicate bits (may be several comparators done in parallel).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5360" data-end="5427">
<p data-start="5362" data-end="5427"><strong data-start="5362" data-end="5377">Register 1:</strong> latch predicates and input key/value (valid bit).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5428" data-end="5521">
<p data-start="5430" data-end="5521"><strong data-start="5430" data-end="5442">Stage 2:</strong> Combine predicates into one-hot selection (priority encoder or tree of logic).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5522" data-end="5588">
<p data-start="5524" data-end="5588"><strong data-start="5524" data-end="5539">Register 2:</strong> latch one-hot selection and any partial results.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5589" data-end="5682">
<p data-start="5591" data-end="5682"><strong data-start="5591" data-end="5603">Stage 3:</strong> Wide <code data-start="5609" data-end="5614">N:1</code> mux (bit-sliced) uses the one-hot select to output the chosen word.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5683" data-end="5766">
<p data-start="5685" data-end="5766"><strong data-start="5685" data-end="5700">Register 3:</strong> final output register (optional, if you want synchronous output).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="5768" data-end="5876">Latency = number of stages; throughput ≈ one decision finished per clock (if no stalls) after pipeline fill.</p>
<h3 data-start="5878" data-end="5923">3.3 Handling control, hazards, and stalls</h3>
<ul data-start="5924" data-end="6530">
<li data-start="5924" data-end="6036">
<p data-start="5926" data-end="6036"><strong data-start="5926" data-end="5940">Valid bit:</strong> accompany data with a <code data-start="5963" data-end="5970">valid</code> bit set by the producer; downstream logic ignores when <code data-start="6026" data-end="6035">valid=0</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6037" data-end="6187">
<p data-start="6039" data-end="6187"><strong data-start="6039" data-end="6049">Stall:</strong> if downstream is busy, assert <code data-start="6080" data-end="6087">stall</code> and freeze upstream registers (use enable signals on registers implemented with NAND-based gating).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6188" data-end="6308">
<p data-start="6190" data-end="6308"><strong data-start="6190" data-end="6200">Flush:</strong> to cancel an in-flight decision (e.g., mispredict), assert <code data-start="6260" data-end="6267">flush</code> to clear <code data-start="6277" data-end="6284">valid</code> or set default outputs.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6309" data-end="6530">
<p data-start="6311" data-end="6530"><strong data-start="6311" data-end="6326">Forwarding:</strong> if the pipeline produces intermediate values that are consumed immediately by next stage, structure registers so needed signals are available without extra cycles or implement bypass paths (extra muxes).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="6532" data-end="6564">3.4 Example timing tradeoffs</h3>
<ul data-start="6565" data-end="6819">
<li data-start="6565" data-end="6689">
<p data-start="6567" data-end="6689">More stages ⇒ lower combinational depth per stage ⇒ higher clock frequency but higher latency and increased register area.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6690" data-end="6819">
<p data-start="6692" data-end="6819">Fewer stages ⇒ less register cost and lower latency, but slower clock or larger combinational gates requiring more NAND levels.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="6821" data-end="6824">
<h1 data-start="6826" data-end="6867">4. Implementation patterns you’ll reuse</h1>
<h3 data-start="6869" data-end="6927">Pattern A — Parallel comparators + one-hot + final mux</h3>
<ul data-start="6928" data-end="7100">
<li data-start="6928" data-end="7018">
<p data-start="6930" data-end="7018">Pros: single-cycle decision once pipeline filled, simple control for final mux (one hot)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7019" data-end="7056">
<p data-start="7021" data-end="7056">Cons: many comparators → large area</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7057" data-end="7100">
<p data-start="7059" data-end="7100">Use when throughput is primary objective.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="7102" data-end="7162">Pattern B — Cascaded comparators + priority mux (serial)</h3>
<ul data-start="7163" data-end="7389">
<li data-start="7163" data-end="7246">
<p data-start="7165" data-end="7246">Pros: uses fewer comparators (early exit), lower gate count for sparse predicates</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7247" data-end="7325">
<p data-start="7249" data-end="7325">Cons: variable delay (worst case long), harder to pipeline for fixed latency</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7326" data-end="7389">
<p data-start="7328" data-end="7389">Use when predicates are skewed or average-case speed matters.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="7391" data-end="7436">Pattern C — Balanced binary decision tree</h3>
<ul data-start="7437" data-end="7632">
<li data-start="7437" data-end="7533">
<p data-start="7439" data-end="7533">Build a binary decision tree from pairwise tests; each internal node is a predicate + 2:1 mux.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7534" data-end="7632">
<p data-start="7536" data-end="7632">Balanced ⇒ <code data-start="7547" data-end="7557">O(log N)</code> levels of predicates &amp; muxes; good for pipelining and predictable latency.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="7634" data-end="7637">
<h1 data-start="7639" data-end="7667">5. Concrete small examples</h1>
<h3 data-start="7669" data-end="7744">Example 1 — <code data-start="7685" data-end="7718">if (x &gt; T) out = A else out = B</code> (single comparator + mux)</h3>
<ul data-start="7745" data-end="7991">
<li data-start="7745" data-end="7809">
<p data-start="7747" data-end="7809">Build <code data-start="7753" data-end="7770">GT = GT_w(x, T)</code> via MSB-first NAND cascade (see §1.4).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7810" data-end="7895">
<p data-start="7812" data-end="7895">Use <code data-start="7816" data-end="7820">GT</code> as <code data-start="7824" data-end="7827">S</code> in the 2:1 NAND mux (§1.2) replicated per bit to select <code data-start="7884" data-end="7887">A</code> or <code data-start="7891" data-end="7894">B</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7896" data-end="7991">
<p data-start="7898" data-end="7991">To pipeline, put <code data-start="7915" data-end="7919">GT</code> and <code data-start="7924" data-end="7927">x</code> into reg stage 1; put final mux on stage 2 and register output.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="7993" data-end="8059">Example 2 — <code data-start="8009" data-end="8048">if P0 then R0 elif P1 then R1 else R2</code> (priority)</h3>
<ul data-start="8060" data-end="8391">
<li data-start="8060" data-end="8074">
<p data-start="8062" data-end="8074"><code data-start="8062" data-end="8073">sel0 = P0</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="8075" data-end="8203">
<p data-start="8077" data-end="8203"><code data-start="8077" data-end="8107">sel1 = NAND(NAND(P0,P0), P1)</code> implemented as: <code data-start="8124" data-end="8145">notP0 = NAND(P0,P0)</code>, <code data-start="8147" data-end="8170">sel1 = AND(notP0, P1)</code> → use NAND recipe to get <code data-start="8196" data-end="8202">sel1</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="8204" data-end="8256">
<p data-start="8206" data-end="8256"><code data-start="8206" data-end="8245">sel2 = NAND(NAND(P0,P0), NAND(P1,P1))</code> (default).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="8257" data-end="8326">
<p data-start="8259" data-end="8326">Use <code data-start="8263" data-end="8268">sel</code> lines to gate <code data-start="8283" data-end="8293">R0,R1,R2</code> and OR them (via NAND networks).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="8327" data-end="8391">
<p data-start="8329" data-end="8391">Pipeline: compute <code data-start="8347" data-end="8352">sel</code> in stage 1; encode and mux in stage 2.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h3 data-start="8393" data-end="8448">Example 3 — 4-way numeric binning (<code data-start="8432" data-end="8435">x</code> into 4 bins)</h3>
<ul data-start="8449" data-end="8826">
<li data-start="8449" data-end="8648">
<p data-start="8451" data-end="8539">Option 1 (parallel): compute <code data-start="8480" data-end="8494">LT0 = (x&lt;t0)</code>, <code data-start="8496" data-end="8510">LT1 = (x&lt;t1)</code>, <code data-start="8512" data-end="8526">LT2 = (x&lt;t2)</code> in parallel.</p>
<ul data-start="8542" data-end="8648">
<li data-start="8542" data-end="8606">
<p data-start="8544" data-end="8606">Convert thresholds into one-hot <code data-start="8576" data-end="8586">sel[3:0]</code> (priority encoder).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="8609" data-end="8648">
<p data-start="8611" data-end="8648">Final 4:1 wide mux picks bin outputs.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="8649" data-end="8826">
<p data-start="8651" data-end="8826">Option 2 (binary tree): compare MSB subsets to route left/right then lower bits; yields <code data-start="8739" data-end="8750">log2(4)=2</code> levels of decisions and 2 pipeline stages (if you register between levels).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="8828" data-end="8831">
<h1 data-start="8833" data-end="8865">6. Practical tips and pitfalls</h1>
<ul data-start="8867" data-end="9748">
<li data-start="8867" data-end="8973">
<p data-start="8869" data-end="8973"><strong data-start="8869" data-end="8885">Bit-slicing:</strong> implement wide operations by bit-slicing single-bit NAND networks; keep wiring regular.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="8974" data-end="9106">
<p data-start="8976" data-end="9106"><strong data-start="8976" data-end="9006">Avoid combinational loops:</strong> when adding registers, ensure no path creates feedthrough unless explicit latch semantics intended.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9107" data-end="9288">
<p data-start="9109" data-end="9288"><strong data-start="9109" data-end="9132">Gate count &amp; depth:</strong> NAND count grows linearly with bitwidth and number of comparators; depth grows with comparator bitwidth and mux tree height. Use pipelining to split depth.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9289" data-end="9479">
<p data-start="9291" data-end="9479"><strong data-start="9291" data-end="9329">One-hot vs binary encoded selects:</strong> one-hot simplifies final selection (just AND and OR per bit) at cost of decoding; binary select requires <code data-start="9435" data-end="9444">log2(N)</code> controls and multi-level mux tree.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9480" data-end="9598">
<p data-start="9482" data-end="9598"><strong data-start="9482" data-end="9494">Testing:</strong> verify with small widths (4 or 8) before scaling; check edge cases (equal values, boundary thresholds).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="9599" data-end="9748">
<p data-start="9601" data-end="9748"><strong data-start="9601" data-end="9626">Registers from NANDs:</strong> design stable D-FF from NAND master-slave pair; add synchronous enable by gating D input with NAND-based logic if needed.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="9750" data-end="9753">
