Although we have chosen to use Nand as our basic building block, other approachel are possible. For example, one can build a complete computer platform using Nol gates alone, or, alternatively, a combination of And Or, and Not gates. These con structive approaches to logic design are theoretically equivalent, just as all the thed rems in the same geometry can be founded on different sets of axioms as alternativ points of departure. The theory and practice of such constructions are covered it standard textbooks about digital design or logic design.
Throughout the chapter, we paid no attention to efficiency considerations such al the number of elementary gates used in constructing a composite gate or the numbe of wire crossovers implied by the design. Such considerations are critically importan in practice, and a great deal of computer science and electrical engineering expertis focuses on optimizing them. Another issue We did not address at all is the physica implementation of gates and chips using the laws of physics, for example, the rol of transistors embedded in silicon. There are of course several such implementatiot options, each having its own characteristics (speed, power requirements, productior cost, etc.). Any nontrivial coverage of these issues requires some background electronics and physics.
1.5
Project
Objective Implement all the logic gates presented in the chapter. The only buildin blocks that you can use are primitive Nand gates and the composite gates that yo will gradually build on top of them.
Resources The only tool that you need for this project is the hardware simulato, supplied with the book. All the chips should be implemented in the HDL languag" specified in appendix A. For each one of the chips mentioned in the chapter, w provide a skeletal .hd1 program (text file) with a missing implementation part. I addition, for each chip we provide a .tst script file that tells the hardware simulatd how to test it, along with the correct output file that this script should generat called . cmp or compare file." Y our job is to complete the missing implementatio parts of the supplied . hd1 programs.
