essage-id="17a54e67-6b19-464c-9014-c931ebad7cab" dir="auto" class="min-h-8 text-message relative flex w-full flex-col items-end gap-2 text-start break-words whitespace-normal [.text-message+&amp;]:mt-5" data-message-model-slug="gpt-5-t-mini"><div class="flex w-full flex-col gap-1 empty:hidden first:pt-[3px]"><div class="markdown prose dark:prose-invert w-full break-words dark markdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="401">Nice — this is the classic “build a computer from NANDs” project description. Below I give a compact, practical, gate-by-gate recipe you can drop into an HDL homework flow (Hack/Nand2Tetris style or any simple structural HDL), plus a suggested development &amp; test order and a few implementation notes (gate counts, verification tips, pipelining hooks). No diagrams — everything is textual and concrete.</p>
<hr data-start="403" data-end="406">
<h1 data-start="408" data-end="460">1 — Minimal NAND-only building blocks (single-bit)</h1>
<p data-start="462" data-end="544">Use these exact recipes as the primitive building blocks for every composite chip.</p>
