<hr data-start="1494" data-end="1497">
<h1 data-start="1499" data-end="1533">2 — Multi-bit (bit-sliced) gates</h1>
<p data-start="1535" data-end="1712"><strong data-start="1535" data-end="1549">Principle:</strong> implement multi-bit gates by replicating single-bit gates across bits (bit-slicing). Keep wiring regular: input buses A[w-1:0], B[w-1:0], produce outputs bitwise.</p>
<ul data-start="1714" data-end="1889">
<li data-start="1714" data-end="1747">
<p data-start="1716" data-end="1747"><code data-start="1716" data-end="1719">w</code>-bit NOT: <code data-start="1729" data-end="1732">w</code> copies of NOT.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1748" data-end="1806">
<p data-start="1750" data-end="1806"><code data-start="1750" data-end="1753">w</code>-bit AND/OR/XOR: <code data-start="1770" data-end="1773">w</code> copies of the single-bit recipe.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1807" data-end="1889">
<p data-start="1809" data-end="1889"><code data-start="1809" data-end="1812">w</code>-bit 2:1 MUX: <code data-start="1826" data-end="1829">w</code> copies of the single-bit mux (sharing the same select <code data-start="1884" data-end="1887">S</code>).</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1891" data-end="1941"><strong data-start="1891" data-end="1914">Gate counts per bit</strong> (using the recipes above):</p>
<ul data-start="1942" data-end="2027">
<li data-start="1942" data-end="1961">
<p data-start="1944" data-end="1961">NOT: 1 NAND / bit</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1962" data-end="1982">
<p data-start="1964" data-end="1982">AND: 2 NANDs / bit</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1983" data-end="2002">
<p data-start="1985" data-end="2002">OR: 3 NANDs / bit</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2003" data-end="2027">
<p data-start="2005" data-end="2027">2:1 MUX: 4 NANDs / bit</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2029" data-end="2032">
<h1 data-start="2034" data-end="2087">3 — N-to-1 multiplexors (multi-way) from 2:1 blocks</h1>
<p data-start="2089" data-end="2140"><strong data-start="2089" data-end="2106">Tree (binary)</strong> method (best for power-of-two N):</p>
<ul data-start="2141" data-end="2315">
<li data-start="2141" data-end="2187">
<p data-start="2143" data-end="2187">If <code data-start="2146" data-end="2155">N = 2^k</code>, build <code data-start="2163" data-end="2166">k</code> levels of 2:1 muxes.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2188" data-end="2315">
<p data-start="2190" data-end="2315">Total 2:1 single-bit mux blocks = <code data-start="2224" data-end="2231">N - 1</code>. For <code data-start="2237" data-end="2240">w</code> bits, total NANDs = <code data-start="2261" data-end="2278">w * 4 * (N - 1)</code> using the 4-NAND per-bit mux recipe.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="2317" data-end="2364"><strong data-start="2317" data-end="2349">Decoder + one-hot + AND + OR</strong> (alternative):</p>
<ul data-start="2365" data-end="2611">
<li data-start="2365" data-end="2408">
<p data-start="2367" data-end="2408">Build k→N decoder (use NAND-based logic).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2409" data-end="2522">
<p data-start="2411" data-end="2522">For each input <code data-start="2426" data-end="2429">i</code>, AND the data with one-hot enable <code data-start="2464" data-end="2469">E_i</code> (bitwise AND), then OR-reduce across inputs per bit.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2523" data-end="2611">
<p data-start="2525" data-end="2611">Usually uses more NANDs but can be convenient if you already have a decoder primitive.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2613" data-end="2616">
<h1 data-start="2618" data-end="2665">4 — Comparators (w-bit unsigned greater-than)</h1>
<p data-start="2667" data-end="2731">MSB-first recursive form (works well for manual NAND expansion):</p>
<span class="katex-display"><span class="katex"><span class="katex-mathml"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo separator="true">,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∧</mo><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="true">‾</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo>∨</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mo fence="false" stretchy="true" minsize="1.2em" maxsize="1.2em">(</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∧</mo><mi>G</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2..0</mn></mrow></msub><mo separator="true">,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2..0</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="true" minsize="1.2em" maxsize="1.2em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">GT_w(a,b) = (a_{w-1} \land \overline{b_{w-1}}) \;\lor\; \big( (a_{w-1} == b_{w-1}) \land GT_{w-1}(a_{w-2..0}, b_{w-2..0})\big)</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class="katex-html" aria-hidden="true"><span class="base"><span class="strut" style="height: 1em; vertical-align: -0.25em;"></span><span class="mord mathnormal">G</span><span class="mord"><span class="mord mathnormal" style="margin-right: 0.13889em;">T</span><span class="msupsub"><span class="vlist-t vlist-t2"><span class="vlist-r"><span class="vlist" style="height: 0.1514em;"><span style="top: -2.55em; margin-left: -0.1389em; margin-right: 0.05em;"><span class="pstrut" style="height: 2.7em;"></span><span class="siz