<p data-start="3976" data-end="4180">Exact netlist depends on clock polarity convention; consult a standard master-slave NAND D-FF schematic. It’s implementable with ~8–12 NANDs depending on whether you include enable and asynchronous reset.</p>
<p data-start="4182" data-end="4351"><strong data-start="4182" data-end="4200">Synthesis tip:</strong> for the HDL project, you can often use a provided register primitive in the simulator; if not, implement master-slave using NANDs and test thoroughly.</p>
<hr data-start="4353" data-end="4356">
<h1 data-start="4358" data-end="4443">6 — Building <code data-start="4373" data-end="4377">if</code>-then pipelines and decision trees (recap into actionable modules)</h1>
<p data-start="4445" data-end="4473"><strong data-start="4445" data-end="4473">Single <code data-start="4454" data-end="4471">if (P) A else B</code></strong></p>
<ul data-start="4474" data-end="4661">
<li data-start="4474" data-end="4521">
<p data-start="4476" data-end="4521">Build <code data-start="4482" data-end="4485">P</code> (comparator, test, or combination).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4522" data-end="4577">
<p data-start="4524" data-end="4577">Feed <code data-start="4529" data-end="4532">P</code> to the <code data-start="4540" data-end="4543">w</code>-bit 2:1 mux to choose <code data-start="4566" data-end="4569">A</code> or <code data-start="4573" data-end="4576">B</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4578" data-end="4661">
<p data-start="4580" data-end="4661">If pipelined: register <code data-start="4603" data-end="4606">P</code>, <code data-start="4608" data-end="4611">A</code>, <code data-start="4613" data-end="4616">B</code> at stage boundary then do mux in next stage.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="4663" data-end="4698"><strong data-start="4663" data-end="4698">Priority chain (<code data-start="4681" data-end="4695">if/elif/else</code>)</strong></p>
<ul data-start="4699" data-end="5002">
<li data-start="4699" data-end="4874">
<p data-start="4701" data-end="4874">Option A (priority encoder): compute <code data-start="4738" data-end="4749">sel0 = P0</code>, <code data-start="4751" data-end="4768">sel1 = ¬P0 ∧ P1</code>, etc., using NAND recipes. Then use decoder-like one-hot selects gated across the data words and OR them.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="4875" data-end="5002">
<p data-start="4877" data-end="5002">Option B (tree): balance <code data-start="4902" data-end="4911">if/elif</code> into binary tree of 2:1 muxes controlled by predicates at internal nodes to get log-depth.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="5004" data-end="5044"><strong data-start="5004" data-end="5044">Decision tree (binary tree of tests)</strong></p>
<ul data-start="5045" data-end="5291">
<li data-start="5045" data-end="5148">
<p data-start="5047" data-end="5148">Each internal node: evaluate predicate <code data-start="5086" data-end="5090">Pi</code> and use a 2:1 mux to select left vs right subtree result.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5149" data-end="5291">
<p data-start="5151" data-end="5291">Pipelining: put registers between levels. Stage 1: evaluate predicates for level 0; Stage 2: evaluate predicates for level 1 and route; etc.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="5293" data-end="5296">
<h1 data-start="5298" data-end="5340">7 — Pipelining, control signals, hazards</h1>
<ul data-start="5342" data-end="5739">
<li data-start="5342" data-end="5440">
<p data-start="5344" data-end="5440">Add <code data-start="5348" data-end="5355">valid</code> with every data packet. <code data-start="5380" data-end="5387">valid</code> is a single bit propagated and used to gate updates.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5441" data-end="5541">
<p data-start="5443" data-end="5541">For stalling: use register-enable (DFF with gated clock or enable gating built with NAND PRAGMAS).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5542" data-end="5596">
<p data-start="5544" data-end="5596">Flush: force <code data-start="5557" data-end="5566">valid=0</code> on pipeline stages to cancel.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5597" data-end="5739">
<p data-start="5599" data-end="5739">For branching/mispredict-like flow, you’ll need flush logic; for pure combinational mapping of <code data-start="5694" data-end="5698">if</code> statements to muxes, flush isn't needed.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="5741" data-end="5744">
<h1 data-start="5746" data-end="5808">8 — HDL project workflow and recommended order (incremental)</h1>
<p data-start="5810" data-end="5893">Implement and test in this order (each step produce an .hdl and wire up .tst/.cmp):</p>
<ol data-start="5895" data-end="6716">
<li data-start="5895" data-end="5933">
<p data-start="5898" data-end="5933"><code data-start="5898" data-end="5907">Not.hdl</code> — test vectors: 0→1, 1→0.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5934" data-end="5972">
<p data-start="5937" data-end="5972"><code data-start="5937" data-end="5946">And.hdl</code> — 2-bit truth table test.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="5973" data-end="6004">
<p data-start="5976" data-end="6004"><code data-start="5976" data-end="5984">Or.hdl</code> — truth table test.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6005" data-end="6050">
<p data-start="6008" data-end="6050"><code data-start="6008" data-end="6017">Xor.hdl</code> / <code data-start="6020" data-end="6028">Eq.hdl</code> — small test vectors.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6051" data-end="6111">
<p data-start="6054" data-end="6111"><code data-start="6054" data-end="6063">Mux.hdl</code> (1-bit 2:1) — test all combinations of S, A, B.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6112" data-end="6224">
<p data-start="6115" data-end="6224"><code data-start="6115" data-end="6126">Multi-bit</code> versions: <code data-start="6137" data-end="6148">Not16.hdl</code>, <code data-start="6150" data-end="6161">And16.hdl</code>, <code data-start="6163" data-end="6174">Mux16.hdl</code> — test with sample vectors (e.g., 16'h00FF etc.).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6225" data-end="6287">
<p data-start="6228" data-end="6287"><code data-start="6228" data-end="6238">DMux.hdl</code>, <code data-start="6240" data-end="6250">Decoders</code> (2→4, 3→8), <code data-start="6263" data-end="6271">OneHot</code> — test selects.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6288" data-end="6324">
<p data-start="6291" data-end="6324"><code data-start="6291" data-end="6296">ALU</code> small pieces (if required).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6325" data-end="6385">
<p data-start="6328" data-end="6385"><code data-start="6328" data-end="6347">Comparator (GT_w)</code> — test edge cases and random vectors.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6386" data-end="6452">
<p data-start="6390" data-end="6452"><code data-start="6390" data-end="6400">Register</code> / <code data-start="6403" data-end="6408">DFF</code> — clock-driven tests if simulator supports.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6453" data-end="6516">
<p data-start="6457" data-end="6516"><code data-start="6457" data-end="6463">MuxN</code> (4:1, 8:1) built from 2:1 tree — test across inputs.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6517" data-end="6594">
<p data-start="6521" data-end="6594"><code data-start="6521" data-end="6536">Decision tree</code> demo: small 4-way numeric binning circuit and test bench.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6595" data-end="6716">
<p data-start="6599" data-end="6716"><code data-start="6599" data-end="6624">Pipelined decision tree</code> — verify latency/throughput by stepping clock and validating outputs and <code data-start="6698" data-end="6705">valid</code> semantics.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<hr data-start="6718" data-end="6721">
<h1 data-start="6723" data-end="6756">9 — Testing &amp; verification tips</h1>
<ul data-start="6758" data-end="7292">
<li data-start="6758" data-end="6852">
<p data-start="6760" data-end="6852"><strong data-start="6760" data-end="6790">Unit test every primitive.</strong> Hard-to-find bugs originate from a bad NOT or inverted clock.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6853" data-end="6938">
<p data-start="6855" data-end="6938"><strong data-start="6855" data-end="6888">Exhaustive small-bit testing.</strong> For 1–3 bit modules, run every input combination.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="6939" data-end="7065">
<p data-start="6941" data-end="7065"><strong data-start="6941" data-end="6963">Randomized testing</strong> for wider buses: run many random vectors and compare against a high-level reference (software model).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7066" data-end="7176">
<p data-start="7068" data-end="7176"><strong data-start="7068" data-end="7096">Check timing of pipeline</strong>: step the simulator clock and track <code data-start="7133" data-end="7140">valid</code> bits to ensure correct propagation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7177" data-end="7292">
<p data-start="7179" data-end="7292"><strong data-start="7179" data-end="7194">Count NANDs</strong>: add comments listing the expected NAND count for the module — helps spot accidental extra gates.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="7294" data-end="7297">
<h1 data-start="7299" data-end="7349">10 — Gate-count &amp; optimization notes (practical)</h1>
<ul data-start="7351" data-end="7894">
<li data-start="7351" data-end="7528">
<p data-start="7353" data-end="7528">The provided recipes are clear, not always gate-optimal. If gate-count is a concern, algebraic factoring can reduce NAND count (common subexpressions, share inverted signals).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7529" data-end="7689">
<p data-start="7531" data-end="7689">OR via De Morgan (3 NANDs) is usually best for clarity; some users implement OR with 2 NANDs plus extra wiring in specific contexts but beware of readability.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7690" data-end="7779">
<p data-start="7692" data-end="7779">For MUX-heavy designs, consider factoring shared <code data-start="7741" data-end="7749">NOT(S)</code> or <code data-start="7753" data-end="7764">NOT(A[i])</code> to save NANDs.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="7780" data-end="7894">
<p data-start="7782" data-end="7894">For FPGA-like targets, the synthesizer maps to LUTs — gate count is less meaningful; aim for regular structures.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="7896" data-end="7899">
<p data-start="7901" data-end="7923">If you want now I can:</p>
<ul data-start="7925" data-end="8338">
<li data-start="7925" data-end="8105">
<p data-start="7927" data-end="8105">(A) Emit <strong data-start="7936" data-end="7958">concrete .hdl text</strong> for <code data-start="7963" data-end="7972">Not.hdl</code>, <code data-start="7974" data-end="7983">And.hdl</code>, <code data-start="7985" data-end="7994">Mux.hdl</code>, and <code data-start="8000" data-end="8011">Mux16.hdl</code> in the Hack/Nand2Tetris HDL style (structural), ready to paste into the simulator and test.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="8106" data-end="8216">
<p data-start="8108" data-end="8216">(B) Produce a <strong data-start="8122" data-end="8149">gate-level NAND netlist</strong> (instance-based) for a 1-bit 2:1 mux and an 8-bit GT comparator.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="8217" data-end="8338">
<p data-start="8219" data-end="8338">(C) Produce a <strong data-start="8233" data-end="8266">3-stage pipelined HDL example</strong> (comparators → encoder → wide mux) including <code data-start="8312" data-end="8319">valid</code> propagation logic.</p>
</li>
</ul>
