arkdown-new-styling"><p data-start="0" data-end="388">Perfect — here’s a <strong data-start="19" data-end="42">progressive roadmap</strong> that generalizes your NAND-to-MUX hardware construction into a full pipeline leading up to <strong data-start="134" data-end="193">Coq/Lean-style propositional and dependent type systems</strong>, with explicit analogies and stepwise abstractions. The goal is to move from <strong data-start="271" data-end="385">low-level Boolean logic → propositional logic → type theory → categorical foundations → dependent type systems</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="390" data-end="393">
<h1 data-start="395" data-end="450"><strong data-start="397" data-end="450">Stage 0 — Primitive logic gates (NAND foundation)</strong></h1>
<p data-start="451" data-end="588"><strong data-start="451" data-end="474">Analog in hardware:</strong> single NAND gate.<br data-start="492" data-end="495">
<strong data-start="495" data-end="515">Conceptual role:</strong> universality; every Boolean function can be built from this primitive.</p>
<ul data-start="590" data-end="786">
<li data-start="590" data-end="651">
<p data-start="592" data-end="651"><strong data-start="592" data-end="606">Objective:</strong> learn the minimal “axioms” of computation.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="652" data-end="786">
<p data-start="654" data-end="677"><strong data-start="654" data-end="675">Hardware analogy:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="680" data-end="786">
<li data-start="680" data-end="727">
<p data-start="682" data-end="727">NOT, AND, OR, XOR, MUX, multi-bit versions.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="730" data-end="786">
<p data-start="732" data-end="786">Build pipelines and decision trees using only these.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="788" data-end="896"><strong data-start="788" data-end="801">Takeaway:</strong> a <strong data-start="804" data-end="844">single primitive + composition rules</strong> is sufficient to construct all combinational logic.</p>
<hr data-start="898" data-end="901">
<h1 data-start="903" data-end="956"><strong data-start="905" data-end="956">Stage 1 — Boolean Algebra / Propositional Logic</strong></h1>
<p data-start="957" data-end="1090"><strong data-start="957" data-end="980">Analog in software:</strong> <code data-start="981" data-end="987">Prop</code> in Coq, <code data-start="996" data-end="1002">Prop</code> or <code data-start="1006" data-end="1012">Type</code> in Lean.<br data-start="1021" data-end="1024">
<strong data-start="1024" data-end="1044">Conceptual role:</strong> first-order logic over atomic propositions.</p>
<ul data-start="1092" data-end="1274">
<li data-start="1092" data-end="1140">
<p data-start="1094" data-end="1140"><strong data-start="1094" data-end="1107">Elements:</strong> atomic propositions <code data-start="1128" data-end="1137">p, q, r</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1141" data-end="1181">
<p data-start="1143" data-end="1181"><strong data-start="1143" data-end="1159">Connectives:</strong> <code data-start="1160" data-end="1163">¬</code>, <code data-start="1165" data-end="1168">∧</code>, <code data-start="1170" data-end="1173">∨</code>, <code data-start="1175" data-end="1178">→</code>.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1182" data-end="1274">
<p data-start="1184" data-end="1274"><strong data-start="1184" data-end="1212">Implementation strategy:</strong> define as <strong data-start="1223" data-end="1271">inductive types / truth-functional semantics</strong>:</p>
</li>
</ul>
