<li data-start="1483" data-end="1658">
<p data-start="1485" data-end="1499"><strong data-start="1485" data-end="1497">Analogy:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1502" data-end="1658">
<li data-start="1502" data-end="1549">
<p data-start="1504" data-end="1549">NAND = universal gate → atomic proposition.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1552" data-end="1607">
<p data-start="1554" data-end="1607">AND, OR, NOT = “built gates” → logical connectives.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1610" data-end="1658">
<p data-start="1612" data-end="1658">MUX / if-then = conditional / proof selection.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="1660" data-end="1663">
<h1 data-start="1665" data-end="1732"><strong data-start="1667" data-end="1732">Stage 2 — Multi-bit / multi-way branching → Proof combinators</strong></h1>
<ul data-start="1733" data-end="2028">
<li data-start="1733" data-end="1806">
<p data-start="1735" data-end="1806"><strong data-start="1735" data-end="1756">Hardware analogy:</strong> multi-bit MUX arrays, multi-way decision trees.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1807" data-end="2028">
<p data-start="1809" data-end="1892"><strong data-start="1809" data-end="1829">Logical analogy:</strong> <strong data-start="1830" data-end="1866">combinators and structural rules</strong> in propositional logic.</p>
<ul data-start="1895" data-end="2028">
<li data-start="1895" data-end="1952">
<p data-start="1897" data-end="1952"><code data-start="1897" data-end="1917">if P then A else B</code> = dependent selection of proofs.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1955" data-end="2028">
<p data-start="1957" data-end="2028"><code data-start="1957" data-end="1971">if-elif-else</code> = priority encoder / sum type selection (<code data-start="2013" data-end="2024">A + B + C</code>).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
