="364">Got it — you want a <strong data-start="20" data-end="91">progressive, conceptual roadmap for building a virtual machine (VM)</strong>, analogous to how we earlier mapped <strong data-start="128" data-end="160">NAND gates → Coq/Lean kernel</strong>, but this time for <strong data-start="180" data-end="250">hardware abstraction, instruction execution, and multi-level logic</strong>. I’ll lay it out <strong data-start="268" data-end="290">from the ground up</strong> in stages, showing how a VM can be constructed from minimal primitives.</p>
<hr data-start="366" data-end="369">
<h1 data-start="371" data-end="407"><strong data-start="373" data-end="407">Stage 0 — Primitive Operations</strong></h1>
<p data-start="408" data-end="454"><strong data-start="408" data-end="425">Lowest level:</strong> individual bit operations.</p>
<ul data-start="456" data-end="677">
<li data-start="456" data-end="524">
<p data-start="458" data-end="524"><strong data-start="458" data-end="483">Conceptual primitive:</strong> NAND (or similar universal operation).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="525" data-end="615">
<p data-start="527" data-end="615"><strong data-start="527" data-end="556">Operations you can build:</strong> NOT, AND, OR, XOR, multi-bit buses, adders, comparators.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="616" data-end="677">
<p data-start="618" data-end="677"><strong data-start="618" data-end="627">Role:</strong> These form the <strong data-start="643" data-end="663">“hardware layer”</strong> for the VM.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="679" data-end="746"><strong data-start="679" data-end="691">Analogy:</strong> your earlier pipeline or multi-bit MUX construction.</p>
<hr data-start="748" data-end="751">
<h1 data-start="753" data-end="791"><strong data-start="755" data-end="791">Stage 1 — Micro-Operations / ALU</strong></h1>
<p data-start="792" data-end="881"><strong data-start="792" data-end="827">Arithmetic and logic unit (ALU)</strong>: performs the basic operations the VM will execute.</p>
<ul data-start="883" data-end="1172">
<li data-start="883" data-end="1015">
<p data-start="885" data-end="900"><strong data-start="885" data-end="898">Examples:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="903" data-end="1015">
<li data-start="903" data-end="946">
<p data-start="905" data-end="946"><code data-start="905" data-end="910">ADD</code>, <code data-start="912" data-end="917">SUB</code>, <code data-start="919" data-end="924">AND</code>, <code data-start="926" data-end="930">OR</code>, <code data-start="932" data-end="937">XOR</code>, <code data-start="939" data-end="944">NOT</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start="949" data-end="978">
<p data-start="951" data-end="978">Bit shifts (<code data-start="963" data-end="968">SHL</code>, <code data-start="970" data-end="975">SHR</code>)</p>
</li>
<li data-start="981" data-end="1015">
<p data-start="983" data-end="1015">Comparisons (<code data-start="996" data-end="1000">EQ</code>, <code data-start="1002" data-end="1006">GT</code>, <code data-start="1008" data-end="1012">LT</code>)</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1016" data-end="1093">
<p data-start="1018" data-end="1093"><strong data-start="1018" data-end="1037">Implementation:</strong> combinational circuits built from stage 0 primitives.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1094" data-end="1172">
<p data-start="1096" data-end="1172"><strong data-start="1096" data-end="1108">Analogy:</strong> hardware “module” → can be composed into larger instructions.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1174" data-end="1242"><strong data-start="1174" data-end="1189">VM insight:</strong> this is the <strong data-start="1202" data-end="1231">core computational engine</strong> of the VM.</p>
<hr data-start="1244" data-end="1247">
<h1 data-start="1249" data-end="1283"><strong data-start="1251" data-end="1283">Stage 2 — Registers / Memory</strong></h1>
<ul data-start="1284" data-end="1500">
<li data-start="1284" data-end="1350">
<p data-start="1286" data-end="1350"><strong data-start="1286" data-end="1300">Registers:</strong> small storage locations for ALU inputs/outputs.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1351" data-end="1440">
<p data-start="1353" data-end="1440"><strong data-start="1353" data-end="1376">Memory abstraction:</strong> arrays of bits / bytes; can be emulated with multi-bit buses.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1441" data-end="1500">
<p data-start="1443" data-end="1500"><strong data-start="1443" data-end="1458">Operations:</strong> <code data-start="1459" data-end="1465">LOAD</code>, <code data-start="1467" data-end="1474">STORE</code>, <code data-start="1476" data-end="1482">MOVE</code>, <code data-start="1484" data-end="1490">PUSH</code>, <code data-start="1492" data-end="1497">POP</code>.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start="1502" data-end="1594"><strong data-start="1502" data-end="1514">Analogy:</strong> pipelines and buses from earlier hardware work become <strong data-start="1569" data-end="1593">data paths in the VM</strong>.</p>
<hr data-start="1596" data-end="1599">
<h1 data-start="1601" data-end="1651"><strong data-start="1603" data-end="1651">Stage 3 — Instruction Set Architecture (ISA)</strong></h1>
<p data-start="1652" data-end="1711">Define a minimal set of instructions your VM can execute.</p>
<ul data-start="1713" data-end="2031">
<li data-start="1713" data-end="1878">
<p data-start="1715" data-end="1738"><strong data-start="1715" data-end="1736">Opcodes examples:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="1741" data-end="1878">
<li data-start="1741" data-end="1769">
<p data-start="1743" data-end="1769">Arithmetic: <code data-start="1755" data-end="1767">ADD r1, r2</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1772" data-end="1801">
<p data-start="1774" data-end="1801">Memory: <code data-start="1782" data-end="1799">LOAD r1, [addr]</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1804" data-end="1849">
<p data-start="1806" data-end="1849">Control flow: <code data-start="1820" data-end="1831">JMP label</code>, <code data-start="1833" data-end="1847">JZ r1, label</code></p>
</li>
<li data-start="1852" data-end="1878">
<p data-start="1854" data-end="1878">I/O: <code data-start="1859" data-end="1867">OUT r1</code>, <code data-start="1869" data-end="1876">IN r1</code></p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="1879" data-end="1954">
<p data-start="1881" data-end="1954"><strong data-start="1881" data-end="1906">Instruction encoding:</strong> fixed-width (e.g., 8-bit opcodes + operands).</p>
</li>
<li data-start="1955" data-end="2031">
<p data-start="1957" data-end="2031"><strong data-start="1957" data-end="1969">VM role:</strong> interprets these instructions using ALU + registers + memory.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2033" data-end="2036">
<h1 data-start="2038" data-end="2066"><strong data-start="2040" data-end="2066">Stage 4 — Control Unit</strong></h1>
<ul data-start="2067" data-end="2527">
<li data-start="2067" data-end="2171">
<p data-start="2069" data-end="2171"><strong data-start="2069" data-end="2082">Function:</strong> interprets instruction words and directs data flow between ALU, registers, and memory.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2172" data-end="2410">
<p data-start="2174" data-end="2191"><strong data-start="2174" data-end="2189">Components:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="2194" data-end="2410">
<li data-start="2194" data-end="2255">
<p data-start="2196" data-end="2255"><strong data-start="2196" data-end="2217">Instruction fetch</strong>: read next instruction from memory.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2258" data-end="2303">
<p data-start="2260" data-end="2303"><strong data-start="2260" data-end="2270">Decode</strong>: identify opcode and operands.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2306" data-end="2352">
<p data-start="2308" data-end="2352"><strong data-start="2308" data-end="2319">Execute</strong>: send signals to ALU / memory.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2355" data-end="2410">
<p data-start="2357" data-end="2410"><strong data-start="2357" data-end="2371">Write-back</strong>: store result in register or memory.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="2411" data-end="2527">
<p data-start="2413" data-end="2527"><strong data-start="2413" data-end="2425">Analogy:</strong> your <strong data-start="2431" data-end="2456">if–then–else pipeline</strong>, MUX trees, and decision logic directly map to instruction decoding.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2529" data-end="2532">
<h1 data-start="2534" data-end="2570"><strong data-start="2536" data-end="2570">Stage 5 — Stack / Program Flow</strong></h1>
<ul data-start="2571" data-end="2869">
<li data-start="2571" data-end="2643">
<p data-start="2573" data-end="2643"><strong data-start="2573" data-end="2583">Stack:</strong> used for function calls, local storage, return addresses.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2644" data-end="2706">
<p data-start="2646" data-end="2706"><strong data-start="2646" data-end="2671">Program counter (PC):</strong> keeps track of next instruction.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2707" data-end="2777">
<p data-start="2709" data-end="2777"><strong data-start="2709" data-end="2723">Branching:</strong> conditional jumps = sum-type / MUX selection logic.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="2778" data-end="2869">
<p data-start="2780" data-end="2869"><strong data-start="2780" data-end="2792">Analogy:</strong> pipelines and conditional MUX trees → conditional branching in VM execution.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="2871" data-end="2874">
<h1 data-start="2876" data-end="2912"><strong data-start="2878" data-end="2912">Stage 6 — VM Abstraction Layer</strong></h1>
<ul data-start="2913" data-end="3275">
<li data-start="2913" data-end="3027">
<p data-start="2915" data-end="3027"><strong data-start="2915" data-end="2941">High-level operations:</strong> simulate multiple instructions per tick, implement virtual memory, or multitasking.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3028" data-end="3137">
<p data-start="3030" data-end="3047"><strong data-start="3030" data-end="3045">Components:</strong></p>
<ul data-start="3050" data-end="3137">
<li data-start="3050" data-end="3094">
<p data-start="3052" data-end="3094">Scheduler: selects “process” to execute.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3097" data-end="3137">
<p data-start="3099" data-end="3137">Interrupt handler: reacts to events.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li data-start="3138" data-end="3275">
<p data-start="3140" data-end="3275"><strong data-start="3140" data-end="3152">Analogy:</strong> multi-stage pipelines + higher-level control logic, like multi-bit buses feeding dependent type structures in Coq analogy.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<hr data-start="3277" data-end="3280">
<h1 data-start="3282" data-end="3317"><strong data-start="3284" data-end="3317">Stage 7 — Runtime Environment</strong></h1>
<ul data-start="3318" data-end="3491">
<li data-start="3318" data-end="3366">
<p data-start="3320" data-end="3366"><strong data-start="3320" data-end="3337">Memory model:</strong> heap, stack, code segment.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3367" data-end="3435">
<p data-start="3369" data-end="3435"><strong data-start="3369" data-end="3393">Built-in operations:</strong> arithmetic, I/O, basic data structures.</p>
</li>
<li data-start="3436" data-end="3491">
<p data-start="3438" data-end="3491"><strong data-start="3438" data-end="3460">VM as interpreter:</strong> runs instructions in a loop:</p>
</li>
</ul>
